## Defining Custom Data Types

A **struct** (structure) groups related data into a single named type. Each piece of data is a **field** — named and typed.

### Struct Syntax

```xiom
pub type Point = {
  x: Float64;
  y: Float64;
}
```

This defines a new type `Point` with two fields:
- `x` of type `Float64` (note: semicolons between fields)
- `y` of type `Float64`

Key syntax rules:
- `pub type Name = { }` — type definition
- Fields separated by **semicolons** (`;`), not commas
- Each field: `name: Type`

### More Struct Examples

```xiom
pub type Person = {
  name: Str;
  age: Int;
  active: Bool;
}

pub type Rectangle = {
  width: Float64;
  height: Float64;
}

pub type Color = {
  r: Int;
  g: Int;
  b: Int;
}
```

### Struct Fields Can Be Any Type

```xiom
pub type Student = {
  name: Str;
  grades: Vec[Int];    // Vec field
  average: Float64;
  enrolled: Bool;
}
```

### Naming Conventions

- Type names: **PascalCase** — `Point`, `UserProfile`, `HttpRequest`
- Field names: **snake_case** — `first_name`, `created_at`
- Use `pub type` to make the struct visible outside the current module

### Why Structs?

Structs group related data so you can pass one value instead of many parameters. They make code self-documenting — the field names describe the data.

